---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';

const pageUrl = new URL('/privacy/', siteConfig.siteUrl).toString();
const privacySchemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'PrivacyPolicy',
    '@id': `${pageUrl}#privacy`,
    url: pageUrl,
    name: '5 Whys Career Studio Privacy Policy',
    description:
      'Privacy policy for 5 Whys Career Studio, covering local drafting, exports, and contact requests.',
    isPartOf: {
      '@id': 'https://5whys.jonathanrreed.com/#website',
    },
    author: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: siteConfig.author.name,
      url: siteConfig.author.url,
    },
  },
];
/** Changed by hand whenever a promise on this page changes. */
const LAST_CHANGED = '13 November 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="How 5 Whys Career Studio handles your drafts, browser storage, exports, and emails."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Privacy', href: '/privacy/' },
  ]}
  structuredData={privacySchemas}
>
  <div class="relative space-y-10 overflow-hidden px-4 pb-16 pt-8 sm:pt-10">
    <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl">
      <PageHero
        eyebrow="Privacy"
        title="Everything you write stays on your device."
        description="None of the tools here need an account, a payment, or a backend server to save your work."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl space-y-6">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last changed {LAST_CHANGED}.</p>
      <article
        class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">What the tools store</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Everything you type, upload, or record stays right in your browser on this device. Your
          drafts, reflections, job postings, and resume save automatically as you work so you can
          come back to them later on the same computer. Practice audio recordings are only kept if
          you click the button to save them. Nothing gets sent to a server. You can open the
          dashboard anytime to see what's stored and clear all of it in one click.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article
        class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">On-device AI</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          A few tools can suggest wording or interview questions. Those suggestions run on your
          device using either plain rules in the page or a local model inside your browser, and
          this site never sends your text to an outside AI service. If your browser comes with its
          own local model and you choose to turn it on, your browser downloads that model from its
          maker while your writing still stays local. No model downloads automatically unless you
          click a button that tells you what it's doing. Wage data and job title lists are loaded
          from this site too, so what you type into a search box isn't sent anywhere.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article
        class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">Exports and contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Anything you export is generated locally for your own use. If you email me about the
          site, I only use that message to reply to you and fix or improve the tools.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article
        class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">Requests</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          If you have a privacy question or want me to delete an email you sent, write to
          <a
            class="font-semibold text-foreground [overflow-wrap:anywhere] hover:text-accent"
            href="mailto:contact@JonathanRReed.com">contact@JonathanRReed.com</a
          >.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-accent mb-5 border-t border-border/40 pt-10">
        Good to know
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        <p>
          Browser storage only lives on the device and browser you used. It's there so you don't
          lose work between sessions on the same computer, not to sync across devices. Clearing
          your browser data, using private mode, or switching computers will wipe or leave behind
          those local drafts.
        </p>
        <p>
          On a shared or lab computer, the next person who opens that browser can visit the
          dashboard and read whatever you left behind. Before you walk away, click "Clear
          everything on this device" on the dashboard, or just use a private window so everything
          disappears when you close it. Backup files you export are plain text, so store them
          somewhere only you can get to.
        </p>
        <p>
          For schools and younger visitors: because there are no accounts and no server-side logs
          of visitor activity, the site doesn't hold any student records. Resumes naturally include
          a name, contact info, and a school, so the resume tools ask for those fields, but
          everything you type goes into your own resume on your own computer and nowhere else.
          Whatever a student writes stays on that machine until they export or clear it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Don't paste confidential employer files, private client records, medical details, or
          financial records into a browser tool unless you're fine with that text sitting on your
          device. Stick to career notes, resume drafts, interview stories, and practice answers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once you export a file or copy text out of the site, it's handled by whatever app you
          open it in. Treat exported files like any other job search document: read through them
          before sharing, strip out details about people who should stay private, and keep backups
          somewhere you trust.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can see the current hosting and infrastructure list on the
          <a class="font-semibold text-foreground hover:text-accent" href="/subprocessors/"
            >subprocessors page</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
